// KNOWN LIMITATION — should verify, currently rejected.
//
// Holding `1/2` at `x` and `1/2` at `y`, a total of `1/1` at x's location forces the
// two locations to coincide. `perm(x.f)` is the Σ-ite summary
// `1/2 + ite(y@addr == x@addr, 1/2, 0)`, so assuming it equals `1/1` does pin the
// gate `y@addr == x@addr` to true. Getting from there to `x == y` needs **location
// injectivity** — `f@addr(p) == f@addr(q) ==> p == q` — which we never state.
// Congruence only runs the other way (`p == q ==> f@addr(p) == f@addr(q)`), and that
// is the direction `contra-congruence` (verify/rewrite.rs) exploits to derive
// DISequality from the non-aliasing axiom. Neither helps here.
//
// This gap was unreachable before the Σ-ite summary landed: no Viper construct can
// equate two locations without equating the receivers, because the surface language
// has no term of location sort (`a.f` in expression position is a *value*).
// `perm(e.f)` is the sole location-indexed observation, and it only became one once
// it started summing symbolically. Silicon never faces this — its chunk matching
// compares receiver terms directly, so the receiver equation IS the gate.
//
// The important half is already covered: the state here is CONSISTENT, so the
// rejection is honest and not a vacuous discharge. That is pinned by
// failing/permissions/perm_sum_is_consistent.vpr, which asserts `false` in exactly
// this state and must fail.
//
// Found 2026-08-09 while making `perm()` sum over the whole location group.

field f: Int

method test(x: Ref, y: Ref) {
    inhale acc(x.f, 1/2) && acc(y.f, 1/2)
    inhale perm(x.f) == 1/1
    assert x == y
}
